## "Review <component>" and "Review the last change"
Only for the approved reviewers above. You check and report; you never fix.
1. What to review: the component named, or every component whose file or test changed since
   `develop` (`git diff --name-only develop`, or the branch you were given).
2. For each one, read its task (`archvow prompt <component>`): description, decisions,
   rules, edge cases, **Must never happen**, invariants. Then read its file and its test.
3. Check what the tools cannot: the code does what the description and every decision say; each
   edge case is handled and tested; tests assert real results, not only "no error"; each
   invariant is really tested; nothing was assumed without `archvow assumption add`; no error is
   swallowed. Run `archvow check`, `ruff check .`, `ruff format --check .`
   and `pytest`; report what fails.
4. Write the report to `reviews/<component>.md` (`reviews/last-change.md` for the last change),
   in exactly this form:

       # Review
       @reviewer   <your tool, as listed above>
       @model      <your model>
       @base       <the branch compared with, or ->
       @components <component>, <component>
       @date       <YYYY-MM-DD>

       ## Findings
       - HIGH <file>:<line> - <what is wrong; the decision, rule or invariant it breaks>
       - MEDIUM <file>:<line> - <...>
       - LOW <file>:<line> - <...>

       ## Questions for the architect
       - <a gap in the spec itself, or: none>

   HIGH: a wrong result, a decision or invariant broken, data lost, a crash. MEDIUM: an edge
   case or invariant without a real test, an unrecorded assumption. LOW: clarity, naming, style.
   Describe each problem in words; never write the fix as code. Nothing found: `- none`.
5. Tell the user the verdict in one line (how many HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW) and where the report is.

{% endif %}This repository is specified with archvow. The spec decides **what exists** and **what must
never happen**; you implement it. `archvow check`, ruff and pytest verify your work - your
own statement that it is correct is not evidence. Working code that breaks these rules is
not done.

## "Implement <component>"
When asked to implement a component, that means all of this, without being told again: read
its task, follow the rules below, write its file with the header and its tests, run
`ruff format .`, `archvow check`, `ruff check .` and `pytest`, and fix everything they report. Asked for
several components ("everything `archvow next` lists as ready"), do them one at a time:
before each one, run `archvow next --base develop` (or the branch the merge request targets, if
you were told another) and implement the first component it lists as ready. Stop when none is
ready. If it says the change budget is full, say so in your report: the rest goes in the next
merge request, so the reviewers get a change small enough to read (ARCH022). Finish with a short
report: files written, tests, anything you had to assume. Commit only when asked.
**A feature is checked first**: asked to implement a feature, or the first component of one, first run
`archvow prompt --feature <feature> --clarify` and do the task it prints. If you recorded anything, stop and
report: a person answers before any code.
**A big spec is cut into tasks** (`archvow show <component>` lists T1, T2...): do one task at a
time, in order. `archvow prompt <component>` prints the next open one; mark each test with what
it tests (`@pytest.mark.decision(3)`, `@pytest.mark.rule("R2")`), which is how archvow sees a task
done; after each task `archvow check` and `pytest` pass, then go on with the next task, until none
is left or the change budget is full.
**Check the branch first** (gitflow; developers create branches, you never do): run
`git branch --show-current`. On `main` or `develop`, or on a branch not named
`feature/<ticket>-<short-name>` (`hotfix/<ticket>-<short-name>` for a fix of production), stop
before writing anything and give the developer the commands, in order:
1. only if `develop` does not exist yet (`git branch -a`): `git switch -c develop main && git push -u origin develop`;
2. then, with the ticket of its task (`@spec`): `git switch -c feature/<ticket>-<short-name> develop`
   (e.g. `git switch -c feature/{{ ticket(1) }}-orders-router develop`).
The Git hook and the CI refuse other names.

## "Draft the spec of <feature>"
{% if mode == 'strict' -%}
When asked to draft the spec of a feature described in plain words, you **propose**; a person
decides. Write no code and run none of the commands below.
{%- else -%}
When asked to draft the spec of a feature described in plain words, you **draft**; a person
decides. Write no code. In mode {{ mode }} you run the `component add`, `rule add` and `question add`
commands below yourself (and `feature add`), each `rule add` and `question add` with `--why "<what in the feature asks for it>"`:
each item is marked as added by the AI until a person keeps it (`component accept`, `rule accept`, an
answer or `question accept`) or removes it.
{%- endif %}
1. Read what exists: `archvow next`, `archvow show <feature>` (each feature and its components), and `archvow catalog` (the
   component types, their layer and the id each one gives).
2. {% if mode == 'strict' %}Answer with one shell block of the commands you propose, in order{% else %}Run, in order{% endif %}: an
   `archvow feature add <feature> -d "<what the product does there>"` when the feature is new (one word for
   what the product does - orders, invoices - never a ticket), then an
   `archvow component add <type> <name> -d "<what it must do>" --feature <feature> --ticket <ticket> -u <components it uses>` for
   each new component (reuse the existing ones, of any feature), then an
   `archvow rule add <component>[,<component>...] "Given <situation>, when <action>, then <result>"`
   for each rule the feature states (`--type <type>` or `--all` when it applies to every component
   of a type, or to all) - one rule, one test. Write the real columns, values and conditions:
   archvow refuses a `<...>` placeholder left in a description or an answer.
{% if mode == 'strict' -%}
3. After the block, list in words what the description does not say (questions for the
   architect) and the risks worth a hazard (`archvow show <component>` lists them once the components
